 ## Overview
-Normal Regularization is a technique to alleviate numerical instability (jitter) of surface normals near the Unsigned Distance Function (UDF) zero-level set, specifically at cusps where the function is not first-order differentiable [S1]. It solves the problem of unreliable gradients leading to inaccurate surface reconstruction in differentiable volume rendering by replacing naively sampled normals with interpolated normals from the neighborhood [S1]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific focus on stabilizing gradients for UDF-based open surface reconstruction during volume rendering, rather than general gradient estimation or post-processing [S1].
+Normal Regularization is a technique to alleviate numerical instability (jitter) of surface normals near the Unsigned Distance Function (UDF) zero-level set, specifically at cusps where the function is not first-order differentiable [S1]. It solves the problem of unreliable gradients leading to inaccurate surface reconstruction in differentiable volume rendering by replacing naively sampled normals with interpolated normals from the neighborhood [S1]. It builds on [SDF Normal Estimation via Finite Differences](4619-sdf-normal-estimation-via-finite-differences.md) and is distinguished from siblings like [Lambertian Shading with Gradient Normals](1229-lambertian-shading-with-gradient-normals.md) and [Gradient-Based Surface Normal Estimation](4261-gradient-based-surface-normal-estimation.md) by its specific focus on stabilizing gradients for UDF-based open surface reconstruction during volume rendering [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A sequence of points $p(t_i)$ along a ray, the UDF $\Psi(\cdot)$, its derivative $\Psi'(\cdot)$ which returns gradients, and a neighborhood size parameter $K$.
-*   **Output:** A regularized normal vector $\mathbf{n}(p(t_i))$ at the query point.
